Date: Wed, 9 Nov 1994 11:02:07 -0500
Subject: [#94-11-142] Democracy [was: BIOSPHERE SURVIVAL: Desirable Information Technology]

fins@access.digex.net (Vigdor Schreibman - FINS) writes

>On Sat, 5 Nov 1994 CurtAdams@aol.com wrote:

> 
>>  A Greek theory of government was that there were four >>types -
democracy, tyranny, monarch, and aristocracy. They >>followed one another in
a characteristic path.  Democracy's >>downfall was that once the people
realized they could take >>what they wanted by voting, they did just that
(i.e., became
>> wolves), until the society collapsed.

>  We have been practicing various forms of aristocracy in this > country
since its origin, and have yet to exercise the 
> principle that "all power is derived from the people." This is > the
Jeffersonian idea that contemporary management science > has validated as the
most effective form of governance.

>  Vigdor 

Prior to Andrew Jackson, when there enfranchisement required property
ownership, yes, you could consider the country an aristocracy, albeit one
with an atypically enourmous aristocratic class.  Since then, though,
"aristocracy" is no longer appropriate as the requirements for voting were no
longer class-based.  Laws and custom denied women and blacks the vote, but
that reflected a racist and sexist democracy, not aristocracy.

Today, of course, anyone who wishes to vote can, provided only that they are
a citizen and over 18.  If that is an aristocracy rather than a democracy,
then "democracy" is unachievable, and in practical political discourse not
meaningful.

Incidentally, although I support of decentralized power structures (in any
organization), I consider contempory management "science" an ill-defined and
inadequately experimental field.  Experts in the field disagree
enthusiastically, and it makes a dubious authority.

