From: sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan)
Date: Wed, 09 Nov 94 14:03:47 EST
Subject: [#94-11-148] Dean Koontz on governmental power

Dean Ray Koontz is a megaselling author of genre twisting books.  He has
been publishing since 1968 but he started regularly generating bestsellers
in the 1980's and 90's with: Strangers, Watchers, Lightning, The Bad Place,
Cold Fire, Hideaway, Dragon Tears, and Mr. Murder.  The book he is currently
working on is called ``Dark Rivers of the Heart''. In an interview in the
November Locus he reveals that the thematic viewpoint of the book is in
accord with political sentiments I have seen expressed repeatedly and with
vehemence on the extropian list and the cypherpunk list.  The general theme
is governmental abuse of power.

Begin Dean Koontz Locus excerpt

``... And my favorite writers were always didactic - John D. MacDonald,
Robert Heinlein.  It's not like I think I have all the answers to life or
something, but I think I'm noticing some things I don't see anybody else
writing about. All the issues in ``Dark Rivers of the Heart'' - asset
forfeiture laws, the fact that the Congress of the United States no longer
writes laws that it is subject to - nobody seems to be writing about them,
and I think they are fascinating themes for fiction.
  ``In this book, you learn who killed Vincent Foster, why we invaded the
compound in Waco, Texas - it deals with issues I'm really upset about. It's
a book about how the government can come and take your property without
charging you with a crime; about high-tech government surveillance;
government intrusions into our lives; government attempts to control the
Information Highway. I find that absolutely frightening.  There's no excuse
for government to be involved. Knopf thinks this is going to be a very
controversial book. There's no element of the fantastic in it, and yet it's
one of the strangest things I've written, because we're living in a world
that almost surpasses anything you can imagine. It takes place today, and
everything in it can happen now.

End of Locus excerpt   

The identification of Foster's ``killer' and the motivation for the Waco
invasion are presumably based on Koontz's avowedly fictionalized scenario.
It sounds like the book might appeal to some extropians and cypherpunks. 

